    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the requirement.\n\nStep 1 — Use a ring buffer and rolling sum.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nclass RollingAverage {\n  private readonly buffer: number[];\n  private sizeUsed = 0;\n  private index = 0;\n  private sum = 0;\n\n  constructor(private readonly capacity: number) {\n    if (capacity <= 0) throw new Error(\"capacity must be positive\");\n    this.buffer = new Array<number>(capacity).fill(0);\n  }\n\n  add(value: number): number {\n    if (this.sizeUsed < this.capacity) {\n      this.buffer[this.index] = value;\n      this.sum += value;\n      this.sizeUsed++;\n    } else {\n      this.sum -= this.buffer[this.index]!;\n      this.buffer[this.index] = value;\n      this.sum += value;\n    }\n\n    this.index = (this.index + 1) % this.capacity;\n    return this.sum / this.sizeUsed;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction rollingAverageUsingSlice(\n  values: readonly number[],\n  size: number,\n): number[] {\n  const result: number[] = [];\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < values.length; i++) {\n    const start = Math.max(0, i - size + 1);\n    const window = values.slice(start, i + 1);\n    result.push(window.reduce((sum, value) => sum + value, 0) / window.length);\n  }\n\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — Example:\ncapacity 3, values 10,20,30,40\naverages: 10, 15, 20, 30.\n\nStep 3 — Complexity:\nRing buffer O(1) per update, O(k) memory.\nSlice/reduce baseline O(nk).\n\nStep 4 — Production takeaway:\nFor high-frequency metrics, bounded memory and incremental updates are usually more important than repeatedly scanning old samples.\n\nStep 5 — Interview takeaway:\nChoose correctness first, then optimize only the actual bottleneck.",
