    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the requirement.\n\nStep 1 — Sort starts and ends separately and sweep them.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nfunction minimumRooms(\n  starts: readonly number[],\n  ends: readonly number[],\n): number {\n  if (starts.length === 0 || starts.length !== ends.length) return 0;\n\n  const sortedStarts = [...starts].sort((a, b) => a - b);\n  const sortedEnds = [...ends].sort((a, b) => a - b);\n\n  let i = 0;\n  let j = 0;\n  let active = 0;\n  let peak = 0;\n\n  while (i < sortedStarts.length) {\n    if (sortedStarts[i]! < sortedEnds[j]!) {\n      active++;\n      peak = Math.max(peak, active);\n      i++;\n    } else {\n      active--;\n      j++;\n    }\n  }\n\n  return peak;\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction minimumRoomsUsingEvents(\n  starts: readonly number[],\n  ends: readonly number[],\n): number {\n  const events = [\n    ...starts.map((time) => [time, 1] as const),\n    ...ends.map((time) => [time, -1] as const),\n  ].sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0] || a[1] - b[1]);\n\n  let active = 0;\n  let peak = 0;\n\n  for (const [, delta] of events) {\n    active += delta;\n    peak = Math.max(peak, active);\n  }\n\n  return peak;\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — Example:\nstarts=[1,2,4], ends=[3,5,6]\nmaximum overlap = 2.\n\nStep 3 — Complexity:\nO(n log n) sorting, O(n) sweep.\nMemory O(n) for copied/sorted arrays/events.\n\nStep 4 — Boundary note:\nWhether equal start/end times overlap determines the tie rule.\n\nStep 5 — Interview takeaway:\nChoose correctness first, then optimize only the actual bottleneck.",
