    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the requirement.\n\nStep 1 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE: count requests per time bucket.\n```ts\ninterface RateLimitState {\n  start: number;\n  count: number;\n}\n\nclass FixedWindowRateLimiter {\n  private readonly windows = new Map<string, RateLimitState>();\n\n  constructor(\n    private readonly limit: number,\n    private readonly windowMs: number,\n  ) {}\n\n  allow(key: string, now = Date.now()): boolean {\n    const current = this.windows.get(key);\n\n    if (!current || now - current.start >= this.windowMs) {\n      this.windows.set(key, { start: now, count: 1 });\n      return true;\n    }\n\n    if (current.count >= this.limit) {\n      return false;\n    }\n\n    current.count++;\n    return true;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN-style wrapper:\n```ts\nfunction canRequest(\n  timestamps: readonly number[],\n  now: number,\n  windowMs: number,\n  limit: number,\n): boolean {\n  const recent = timestamps.filter(\n    (timestamp) => now - timestamp < windowMs,\n  );\n\n  return recent.length < limit;\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — Example:\nlimit=3 per 1000ms\nrequests at 100,200,300 → allowed\nrequest at 400 → blocked\nrequest at 1200 → new window → allowed\n\nStep 3 — Complexity:\nMap-based fixed window allow() is O(1) average.\nThe timestamp-filter baseline is O(n).\n\nStep 4 — Important:\nFor distributed APIs, in-memory frontend state is not a security boundary. Server-side rate limiting is required for enforcement.\n\nStep 5 — Interview takeaway:\nChoose correctness first, then optimize only the actual bottleneck.",
